  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single case study of Beijing and rely on mobile phone location data as a proxy for rider demographics and trajectories.",
      "what": "Using mobile phone data from 8.3 million users in Beijing, China, this study reconstructs segment-by-segment transit trajectories to measure socioeconomic diversity at both destinations and along transit corridors. The analysis reveals that moderate improvements in spatial accessibility correlate with lower ridership diversity, and that buses experience a drop in diversity during peak hours while subways maintain high diversity throughout the day.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that expanding transit access does not automatically foster social mixing, revealing instead how network structure and transit mode create distinct spatial and temporal patterns of socioeconomic sorting."
    }
